The issue lies in the incorrect format specifier used in your `scanf` function for reading the float value.

Here’s the problematic line:
```cpp
scanf("%d %ld %c %float %lf", &a, &b, &c, &d, &e);
```

### ❌ Problem:
- `%float` is **not a valid format specifier** in `scanf`.

### ✅ Fix:
Replace `%float` with `%f`, which is the correct specifier for `float` in `scanf`.

### Corrected Code:
```cpp
scanf("%d %ld %c %f %lf", &a, &b, &c, &d, &e);
```

### Additional Suggestion:
Remove extra spaces from the format strings in `printf` if you want the output to match exactly as required:
```cpp
printf("%d\n", a);
printf("%ld\n", b);
printf("%c\n", c);
printf("%.3f\n", d);
printf("%.9lf\n", e);
```

Once these changes are made, your program will produce the exact required output.
